body:
## Problem

Observation serialization is bounded by bytes and truncates **blind, depth-first** (`packages/e2e/src/agent/observation.ts` `prepareObservation`):

```ts
const emit = (node: SemanticNode, depth: number): void => {
  if (truncated) return;
  const line = formatNode(node, depth, redact, options.testIdAttribute);
  const size = encoder.encode(`${line}\n`).byteLength;
  if (lines.length > 0 && bytes + size > budget) {
    truncated = true;
    return;
  }
  bytes += size;
  lines.push(line);
  for (const child of node.children ?? []) emit(child, depth + 1);
};
emit(observation.tree, 0);
if (truncated) lines.push(TRUNCATION_MARKER);
```

Four consequences on real pages:

- **The cut is positional, not relevance-based.** The model gets the top of the DOM and silently loses everything past the budget. On a long feed or table, the region the test cares about is exactly what gets dropped.
- **The model cannot recover.** `[observation truncated at the resolved observation byte limit]` says *that* something was dropped, not *what*, *how much*, or *where*. There is no affordance to reveal it, so the agent has no reason to scroll.
- **The budget is invisible.** `agent.maxObservationBytes` defaults to 1 MiB (spec 05-config.md). Neither the model nor the report sees how much of it an observation consumed, so a pathologically large tree is only visible as cost.
- **We only count bytes.** `report-1` `stepMetrics` has `observationBytes`/`contextBytes`/`ledgerBytes` but no node counts, so "this page produced 40k nodes" is unobservable after the fact.

## Proposal

Two additive changes. No new driver capability, no change to the locator AST, the action contract, or the security envelope.

### 1. Viewport-scoped semantic trees

Scope the tree the model sees to the captured viewport, and replace elided off-screen runs with **countable, directional placeholders** instead of dropping them:

```
#c12 list "Search results"
  #n41 listitem "Wireless keyboard — $49"
  #n42 listitem "Mechanical keyboard — $129"
  [37 items hidden below in #c12]
```

Better than a byte cutoff because it is:

- **bounded by relevance, not document order** — what the user can see survives, what they cannot is summarised
- **actionable** — the placeholder names its container and direction, so the next move is `scrollIntoView`/swipe on `#c12` rather than an unexplained wall
- **honest** — the count is exact, so the model can tell "3 more" from "3000 more" and decide whether scrolling is worth a step
- **composable** — byte truncation stays as the hard backstop; scoping just means we rarely reach it

Constraints:

- Model-input scoping only. Addressability MUST NOT change: refs inside an elided region must still resolve after the model scrolls.
- Prefer deriving elision **in the runner from the already-captured tree** (`rect` + `viewport`) over in-page DOM mutation. That sidesteps reversibility entirely. If we do go in-page, it must restore prior state exactly — including pre-existing `aria-hidden` values — and must not perturb layout. Worth prototyping both and comparing fidelity.
- Container heuristics stay conservative: scroll extent meaningfully exceeds client extent, overflow actually scrollable, enough children for elision to pay for itself. Tune against `packages/testbed` and the public-sites suite.
- Applies per document; frame capture already shares the observation node budget (spec 09-drivers.md), so nested frames scope independently via `framePath`.
- `fullPage`-style captures opt out.

### 2. Observation stats, surfaced twice

```ts
interface ObservationStats {
  readonly lines: number;
  readonly bytes: number;
  readonly estimatedTokens: number;
  readonly totalNodes: number;
  readonly visibleNodes: number;
  readonly elidedNodes: number;
  readonly addressableRefs: number;
  readonly truncated: boolean;
}
```

- **Into `report-1`**: extend `stepMetrics` with node/elision counts beside the existing byte fields. Turns "which page blows up our observation budget" into a query, and gives change 1 a regression signal.
- **Into model input**: one compact line in the serialized observation. Making budget consumption legible to the consumer is cheap and gives the model grounds to narrow scope instead of re-observing a huge tree repeatedly.

`estimatedTokens` is explicitly an estimate (fixed chars-per-token divisor is fine). Not authoritative, not for cost accounting — real token counts stay with the model adapter.

## Touch points

- `spec/09-drivers.md`: observation section — viewport scoping, placeholder semantics, no-side-effect requirement
- `spec/api/driver.d.ts`: stats on the prepared observation. Placeholders should be **serialization-only**, not a `SemanticNode` variant, so `driver-1` is unchanged
- `spec/schema/report-v1.schema.json`: extend `stepMetrics`
- `spec/05-config.md`: scoping option beside `agent.maxObservationBytes`
- `spec/13-reporting.md`: document the new metrics
- `packages/e2e/src/agent/observation.ts`: scoping + stats
- `packages/e2e/src/report/*`: thread the new metrics
- conformance vectors land with the behavior, per PLAN.md engineering rules

## Acceptance

- Placeholder counts are exact, and every ref in an elided region resolves after the model scrolls its named container
- Elision leaves zero observable trace on the page under test (fixture with pre-existing `aria-hidden` + custom scroll behaviour)
- Measured observation-byte reduction on long-page testbed fixtures with no loss of task success
- Blind byte truncation is not reached on any testbed fixture at default config
- New `stepMetrics` fields validate against `report-1` for every generated report

## Notes

- Open decision: on by default, or opt-in for v0? Defaulting on is the right outcome for tokens but changes agent behaviour suite-wide, which touches the Phase 2 exit gate (100 clean repetitions of the mixed-tier suite).
- The elided-count placeholder is also a useful diagnostic on its own — a step that fails while a placeholder reported 3000 hidden items is a different bug report than one that fails on a fully visible page.

## What and why

- `AgentError` is exported from `@e2edev/e2e`, but the realm-safe check `isAgentError` was only on `@e2edev/e2e/agent`. Test files load in their own module realm (`packages/e2e/src/collect/registry.ts`, the `realmSlot` comment), so `instanceof AgentError` can be false for an error the runner threw; `packages/e2e/src/agent/error.ts` keys identity on a global symbol for that reason.
- The docs said "check `code` rather than using `instanceof`" without naming the function that makes it possible from the test entrypoint.
- `isAgentError` now sits next to `AgentError` on the main entrypoint, `RUNTIME_EXPORTS` in `packages/e2e/src/config/diagnose.ts` lists it (the unit test keeps that list equal to the module), and errors.mdx shows the idiom.

```ts
import { isAgentError } from '@e2edev/e2e';

try {
  await agent.act('upgrade the workspace to Pro');
} catch (error) {
  if (isAgentError(error) && error.code === 'STEP_TIMEOUT') {
    // the step ran out of time; the message carries the last judgment
  }
  throw error;
}
```

