## What and why

- The runner routed every `provider/model-id` string through the Vercel AI Gateway without saying so, and `E2E_MODEL_ENDPOINT` claimed to reach "any OpenAI-compatible endpoint" while the gateway client posts to its own `/language-model` route. Neither belongs in a provider-agnostic runner.
- `agent.model`, `agent.visionModel`, and `createAgent({ model })` now take an AI SDK model instance only. A string is `INVALID_CONFIG` naming the constructor to write. `ModelConfig`, `E2E_MODEL`, `E2E_VISION_MODEL`, `E2E_MODEL_API_KEY`, `E2E_MODEL_ENDPOINT`, and the `AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY` fallback are gone; the runner constructs no model and reads no model variable.
- `e2e init` asks which gateway agent steps use instead of a yes/no on AI: Vercel AI Gateway, OpenRouter, an OpenAI-compatible endpoint (prompts for the URL), or none. It adds that provider package and writes the import and constructor into the config. `--yes` picks the Vercel AI Gateway and still writes it out. Presets live in `packages/e2e/src/cli/init/gateways.ts`; adding a gateway is one entry.
- Credentials are the provider's business: `gateway()` reads `AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY`, `openrouter()` reads `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`. There is no `E2E_*` key variable, on purpose: the runner never sees the key, so it has nothing to name, and one variable for every provider could not serve a config that uses two. A missing key is the provider's own error on the first agent step; only "no model at all" is the run-level `MODEL_UNAVAILABLE`.
- A new guide, [Models and credentials](docs/models.mdx), says which package constructs which model, which variable each reads, how to pass a key explicitly, how several models share one config, and which error surfaces where. The quickstart, agents page, CI page, and environment reference point at it.
- Reports record the instance's provider and model id, `readCost` also reads OpenRouter's usage accounting, and telemetry gains `model_gateway` (the AI SDK provider that served the first model-backed step).

What `init` writes for each choice:

```ts
// Vercel AI Gateway
import { gateway } from 'ai';
agents: { default: createAgent({ model: gateway('openai/gpt-5.4-mini'), system: '...' }) }

// OpenRouter
import { openrouter } from '@openrouter/ai-sdk-provider';
agents: { default: createAgent({ model: openrouter('openai/gpt-5.4-mini'), system: '...' }) }

// OpenAI-compatible endpoint
import { createOpenAICompatible } from '@ai-sdk/openai-compatible';
agents: {
  default: createAgent({
    model: createOpenAICompatible({
      name: 'openai-compatible',
      baseURL: 'http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1',
      // apiKey: process.env.LLM_API_KEY,
    }).chatModel('gpt-5.4-mini'),
    system: '...',
  }),
}
```

Migration, from the changeset: `model: 'openai/gpt-5.4-mini'` becomes `model: gateway('openai/gpt-5.4-mini')` from `ai`, and the key moves to the variable the provider reads (`AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY` for the gateway). The testbed and web-benchmark configs build their models with `gateway()` and keep `E2E_MODEL` as their own override; the workflows read the `AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY` repository secret, so the old `E2E_MODEL_API_KEY` secret is unused after merge.

Not exercised live: no OpenRouter or Vercel key is available on the machine this was built on. The OpenRouter and OpenAI-compatible scaffolds are load-tested (the generated config resolves to an instance) but have not run against a real endpoint.

