- [ ] Engine contract change (`e2e/engine`): changesets for `e2e`, `@e2edev/playwright`, and `@e2edev/agent-device`.

Playwright users structure every test with `test.step`, and page objects wrap their calls in it. `TestAPI` had no equivalent. Every `screen`/`expect`/`agent` call is already a recorded step, so the runtime work is a named nesting node in the report.

```ts
test('shows the dashboard', async ({ app, screen }) => {
  await app.open('/login');
  await new LoginPage(screen).signIn(credentials.user('member'));
  const cards = await test.step('count the cards', () => screen.getByRole('article').count());
  expect(cards).toBeGreaterThan(0);
});

class LoginPage {
  signIn(user: Credential) {
    return test.step(`sign in as ${user.username}`, async () => {
      await this.screen.getByLabel('Email').fill(user.username);
      await this.screen.getByLabel('Password').fill(user.password);
      await this.screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).tap();
    });
  }
}
```

- New step kind `test`, api `test.step`, title as label. The timeline stays flat in start order; each step the body opens carries the enclosing step's id in a new optional `parent` field (`StepRecord`, `ReportStep`, `report-1` schema). Consumers that ignore `parent` see every step as before.
- Step progress identity carries `parentStepId`.
- The recorder is reached through the published attempt, the slot `expect.poll` and `test.skip` use (`packages/e2e/src/internal/test-step.ts`). Outside a running test: `COLLECTION_ERROR`. Title follows the test title rule.
- A body that returns while a step it called is still running fails with `STEP_NOT_AWAITED`, naming the step: `test.step "careless" returned before locator.tap "Save" finished`. A body that throws keeps its error and the abandoned child is failed beside it (`packages/e2e/src/run/steps.ts`, `abandonChildren`).
- `failedStepOf` picks the innermost failed step, so the run summary and the failure page point at the call, not the wrapper. The failure page indents steps by depth.
- `list` reporter: an open-step stack per running pair (`packages/e2e/src/report/list-model.ts`). `test.step` rows print like agent rows; rows inside one nest under it and fold out when it ends, so the header lands above them in CI logs too.
- Telemetry gains `steps_test` through `STEP_KINDS`.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  B[test body] --> S["test.step 'sign in'"]
  S --> F1["locator.fill (parent: sign in)"]
  S --> F2["locator.fill (parent: sign in)"]
  S --> T["locator.tap (parent: sign in)"]
  B --> E["expect.toBeVisible"]
```

CI-mode list output from the testbed:

```
   ✓ steps group the calls they wrap > test.step "read the landing page" 18ms
   ✓ steps group the calls they wrap > test.step "visit todos" 36ms
     ✓ steps group the calls they wrap > test.step "follow the link" 36ms
```

Left out: Playwright's `{ box, timeout }` step options and `test.step.skip`. Reads (`count()`, `textContent()`) are not steps, so they do not appear under a `test.step` either.

