- `EngineInitInfo` gains `log(line)`, the progress line `EnginePrepareInfo` and `EngineFinishInfo` already carry. Until now an engine had no way to surface a fact that only exists once a worker is up: the URL where a hosted browser or device can be watched live, which simulator a slot got, a version it detected. Writing it to the worker's stderr lands underneath the live status block.
- The line rides the same `notice` run event `prepare` uses. Run events are not one of the wire formats under `packages/e2e/schema`, so no schema or fixture changes.
- The executor prefixes the line with the target and slot, `ios worker 1: ...`, before it leaves the worker. A `notice` outside a setup step has nothing else naming where it came from: the list reporter prints the message alone, and two targets with two workers each would otherwise be indistinguishable.
- One new worker-to-runner IPC message, `notice`, fire-and-forget like `progress`. The in-process worker and a standalone attempt (`e2e mcp`) route through the same `TargetExecutor` callback, so every transport ends at the same sink. A line logged after the worker's channel closed is dropped; the doc comment says so.
- `log` is required on the info object. The runner always supplies it and engines only read it, so an optional would only make engine code guard against a case that cannot happen. Every test helper that builds an `EngineInitInfo` by hand passes a no-op. Additive, SPI version stays 1.

```ts
export interface EngineInitInfo {
  readonly runId: string;
  readonly targetName: string;
  readonly projectRoot: string;
  readonly app: EngineAppInfo;
  readonly env: Readonly<Record<string, string | undefined>>;
  readonly headed: boolean;
  readonly workerSlot: number;
  readonly signal: AbortSignal;
  /**
   * Reports one line of progress. The runner streams it as a `notice` run
   * event, prefixed with the target and worker slot, so it reaches the
   * reporter and every host sink instead of being written to a worker's
   * stderr underneath the live status block. ...
   * Meant for `init` itself; a line logged later is still delivered while
   * the worker lives and dropped once it has exited.
   */
  readonly log: (line: string) => void;
}
```

```ts
async init(info) {
  const lease = await pool.lease(info.workerSlot);
  info.log(`watch live at ${lease.viewerUrl}`);
}
```

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A["engine init: info.log(line)"] --> B["TargetExecutor: prefix target + slot"]
  B --> C{transport}
  C -- worker process --> D["IPC notice message"]
  C -- in-process --> E["TargetWorker host.emit"]
  D --> F["scheduler onNotice"]
  E --> F
  F --> G["runner: notice run event"]
  B -- standalone attempt --> G
  G --> H["reporter + host sinks"]
```

Code refs:

- `packages/e2e/src/engine/index.ts:492` the contract, mirrored in `docs/reference/engine.mdx`
- `packages/e2e/src/run/execute.ts:232` where the prefix is composed
- `packages/e2e/src/run/worker/protocol.ts:169` `NoticeMessage`
- `packages/e2e/src/run/worker/session.ts:131`, `packages/e2e/src/run/scheduler.ts:585`, `packages/e2e/src/run/runner.ts:723` the worker-process path
- `packages/e2e/src/run/standalone.ts:122` the standalone attempt
- `packages/e2e/tests/integration/engine-target.test.ts:472` in-process worker, `:500` worker process over IPC

