- `web({ browser })` takes a `BrowserProvider` next to a browser name, the seam `mobile({ device })` already has with `DeviceProvider`. `connect` stays as the low-level resolver; the provider rides the same CDP attach and adds the lifecycle `connect` has no hooks for, so a host no longer wraps the whole engine to learn when to delete a browser it pays for by the minute.
- `scope: 'worker'` (default): `prepare` leases one browser per slot, all slots at once, and holds every lease granted before the outcome is known, so a slot that fails to lease fails the run and the others are still released in `finish`. Leases reach workers through `E2E_WEB_BROWSERS_<TARGET>_<DIGEST>` as declared fields only, capped at 16 KiB, decoded field by field.
- A worker whose browser drops leases a replacement itself at the next attempt start (same slot, `log` discarded) and releases it in `dispose`. The dead prepare-time lease stays with the runner's `finish`: each lease is released exactly once, by the side that acquired it, so a `release` that deletes a session never runs twice for one id. `acquire` therefore runs in worker processes too, and the docs say a provider must not depend on runner-process state.
- `scope: 'attempt'`: `startAttempt` leases with an `attemptId` and attaches through the existing persistent-context path (`reconnectEndpoint`, else `cdpEndpoint` again, checked to be the same browser); `endAttempt` releases on every path, a failed start included, and is idempotent. `headers` and `basicAuth` are `INVALID_CONFIG`, as with `connect.reconnectEndpoint`. `prepare` hands the workers the slot count alone, so `request.slots` means the same thing in both scopes.
- A provider implies chromium. `browser: provider` together with `connect`, an unknown `scope`, or a provider missing `name`, `acquire`, or `release` fail at config load. A target with `slots === 0` leases nothing.
- Docs in the same change: a "Hosted browsers" section in `docs/browser.mdx` with a typechecked example against a generic session API (create a session, poll until it exposes a DevTools URL, stop it), the `browser` option in `docs/reference/web.mdx`, the skill's setup reference, the package README. Changeset: `@e2edev/web` minor.

```ts
export interface BrowserProvider {
  readonly name: string;
  /** `worker` (default): one browser per worker slot for the run. `attempt`: a fresh browser per attempt. */
  readonly scope?: 'worker' | 'attempt';
  acquire(request: BrowserRequest): Promise<BrowserLease>;
  release(lease: BrowserLease, context: BrowserReleaseContext): Promise<void>;
}

export interface BrowserRequest {
  readonly runId: string;
  readonly targetName: string;
  readonly slot: number;
  readonly slots: number;
  /** Present only for a per-attempt lease. */
  readonly attemptId?: string;
  readonly env: Readonly<Record<string, string | undefined>>;
  readonly signal: AbortSignal;
  readonly log: (line: string) => void;
}

export interface BrowserLease {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly cdpEndpoint: string;
  /** Per-attempt leases only; `cdpEndpoint` is used again when absent. */
  readonly reconnectEndpoint?: string;
}
```

