## What and why

- `mobile({ launchArguments, permissions })` ride every fresh launch of the pinned app: `app.open()`, `app.restart()`, `app.clearState()`. Both migration tables listed them as missing. The warm-up in `prepare` and a foreground-only `device.openApp` take neither: an app already running keeps its process, and `app.open()` relaunches it with them.
- `device.openApp(app, { relaunch, launchArguments, permissions })` does the same for one launch of any app.
- `device.clearKeychain()` maps to agent-device's `reset-keychain`: the whole simulator's keychain, which `app.clearState()` leaves alone. iOS only; Android is `UNSUPPORTED_CAPABILITY` before any device command.
- Permissions are set before the launch, never after: iOS terminates a running app whose permission changed, Android one whose permission was revoked. agent-device sets a permission on the app its session is on, and only an open puts a session there (no `settings permission --app` upstream, 0.21.14 and main). The warm-up's open in `prepare` does that under the very session the worker resumes, and the binding it hands the worker names the app, so a worker's launches preset the permissions straight away. A run without `prepare`, the first launch after `closeApp`, and an app the session never opened get one foreground open first: the one process start no permission can precede, and the relaunch after it has them. `app.clearState()` puts them back, since the clear resets them with the data.
- `deny` differs by platform, and the option docs say so: iOS records the refusal and the app is told no without a prompt; Android revokes, and the OS asks again when the app requests the permission.
- The trace cache keys on the declared `identity`, not on the launch options: arguments that select a build mode change what the app is, and the docs tell each such mode to declare its own `identity` so recordings stay apart.
- Prose that still said the engine opens the app fresh per attempt (gone since #499) is corrected where these files were touched: guide, reference, both migration tables, README, skill, two JSDoc lines, the `explore` comment.
- Not here, from the same assessment: the warm-up's `apps.open` could become agent-device's `prepare ios-runner`, and `explore` on a device that declares `restart` should launch.

```ts
const iphone = mobile({
  platform: 'ios',
  app: 'com.example.app',
  launchArguments: ['-e2e', 'YES'],
  permissions: { camera: 'grant', notifications: 'deny', location: 'reset' },
});

test('the scanner opens without a prompt', async ({ app, screen }) => {
  await app.open(); // camera granted before the app starts
  await screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Scan' }).tap();
  await expect(screen.getByText('Point at a code')).toBeVisible();
});

test('a refused camera shows the fallback', async ({ device, screen }) => {
  await device.openApp('com.example.app', { relaunch: true, permissions: { camera: 'deny' } });
  await screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Scan' }).tap();
  await expect(screen.getByText('Camera access is off')).toBeVisible();
});

await device.clearKeychain(); // then app.open(): signed out
```

