- The session store named a file `<target>--<name>.json`. Target `a--b` with session `c` and target `a` with session `b--c` are both allowed by the name rules (`[A-Za-z0-9_.-]`) and both landed in `a--b--c.json`, so the second save overwrote the first.
- Repro: two such setup tests with `workers: 2`. Both pass, then the consumer of the overwritten session fails with `SESSION_MISMATCH` (exit 2). With one worker it works by accident, the first session is consumed before the overwrite. The envelope identity check held throughout, no foreign session was ever decrypted; this is availability only.
- The name now ends in the first 16 hex of `sha256(target + "\0" + name)`, which is what tells two pairs apart. In front of it, each part escaped on its own (`%XX` for every byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9_]`) and cut to 40 characters, as a readable prefix: `web--member-3f9a....json`. The escape keeps a path separator or `..` segment out of the file name; the cut keeps a 128-character session name with any target name at 104 bytes before the 21-byte atomic-write suffix, under the 255-byte component limit.
- Sessions are per run and deleted at cleanup, so there is nothing to migrate. `packages/e2e/src/run/sessions.ts` is the only place a path is derived from these names; the `load` memo key already used `\0`.

```ts
// packages/e2e/src/run/sessions.ts
private filePath(targetId: string, name: string): string {
  const digest = createHash('sha256').update(`${targetId}\0${name}`).digest('hex').slice(0, 16);
  return path.join(this.directory, `${fileNamePart(targetId)}--${fileNamePart(name)}-${digest}.json`);
}
```

