- Repro: on a button with no such attribute, `expect(button).toHaveAttribute('constructor')` passed, `.not.toHaveAttribute('constructor')` failed with `attribute "constructor" undefined`, and `getAttribute('constructor')` returned a function instead of `null`. Same for `toString`, `__proto__`, `hasOwnProperty`.
- Cause: the attribute map is a plain object and both readers did `node.attributes?.[name]`, so a name that is also an `Object.prototype` member answered the member.
- Fix: one core helper does an own-property lookup for both readers, `packages/e2e/src/locator/engine.ts` `attributeOf(node, name)`. An absent attribute is `null` whatever its name; a real `constructor="x"` still reads as its string.
- The web reader builds the map with `Object.create(null)` (`packages/web/src/in-page/read-semantics.ts`) so the same holds in-page.

```ts
export function attributeOf(node: SemanticNode, name: string): string | null {
  const attributes = node.attributes;
  if (attributes === undefined || !Object.hasOwn(attributes, name)) return null;
  return attributes[name] ?? null;
}
```

- One limit worth knowing: Playwright's argument serializer drops a `__proto__` key on the Node side, so a `__proto__="x"` attribute cannot be read through the web engine at all. Absence for it is still `null`. The browser-backed test in `web-platform.test.ts` asserts presence for `constructor` and absence for all three; the unit test covers `__proto__` presence against a fake node.

