## What and why

- Audit B39. Repro: three list items, one reads `Deleted item`. `not.toContainText(['Deleted item'])` passed, and `toContainText(['Alpha', 'Gamma'])` failed on `Alpha`, `Beta`, `Gamma`. Playwright does the opposite on both.
- Cause: the list form shared `toHaveText`'s rule, so it needed the match count to equal the list length (`packages/e2e/src/expect/async.ts`, `textListMatcher`). A count mismatch made the condition false, and the negation passed on that.
- Fix: `toContainText([...])` now uses Playwright's `to.contain.text.array` rule: each entry has to be contained by a distinct match, in list order. It scans greedily and allows extra matches. `toHaveText([...])` still needs the exact count.

```ts
function matchesSubsequence(nodes, patterns, satisfies): boolean {
  let next = 0;
  for (const node of nodes) {
    if (next === patterns.length) break;
    if (satisfies(node, patterns[next]!)) next += 1;
  }
  return next === patterns.length;
}
```

- Tests: unit tests in `locator-basics.test.ts` cover a subsequence, negation, ordering, more entries than matches, duplicate entries, and the empty list. Integration tests: one scripted-engine case where negated `not.toContainText(['runner'])` fails, plus a real-browser subsequence and an out-of-order negation in `web-platform.test.ts`. All of them failed on main.
- Docs: `reference/expect.mdx`, `migrate/playwright.mdx`, `skills/e2e/references/writing-tests.md`, and the `toContainText` JSDoc.

