## What and why

- Audit B42. Repro: trace on, fill a `Secret` into an ordinary visible text field. `app.screenshot()` is `POLICY_DENIED`, but the trace ZIP keeps `screencast/page@...jpeg` frames showing the value, and the artifact says `redaction: 'complete'`.
- Cause: `redactTraceArchives` rewrites text entries only. A binary entry is dropped only when it holds the secret's bytes, so a JPEG that draws the secret gets through, and `complete` covers text only.
- Fix: redaction only runs on a pixel-tainted session, so `redactArchive` now drops the screencast (`packages/e2e/src/run/trace-redaction.ts`):
  - every entry under `screencast/`, by name, whether a record references it or not;
  - every `screencast-frame` record in a `.trace` event stream, via `withoutFrameRecords`. App resources are never read as trace records;
  - any other entry a frame record names (the pre-format-9 `sha1` form under `resources/`).
- Actions, DOM snapshots, and network stay; the viewer just has no filmstrip. Untainted traces are not touched.
- Why drop rather than label: #561 added `redaction` to `StoredArtifact`, so labelling a tainted trace `incomplete` would now reach stores. Dropping is still better: the secret never lands on disk (the invariant is that secrets never reach artifacts; video is the one opt-in exception), and a store that exports only vouched-for artifacts keeps the trace instead of losing it for every signed-in test.

