### What happened

A visible send icon next to a message input is omitted from the semantic observation when it is implemented as an unnamed `div` containing an inline SVG. A DOM visibility assertion passes, but a text-only `agent.assert` cannot verify the same visible UI.

The smallest reproduction below was run locally with `gpt-6-luna`. It produces **1 failed / 1 passed**:

- Original custom control: `expect(browser.locator('#send')).toBeVisible()` passes; `agent.assert(..., { vision: false })` returns `ASSERTION_INCONCLUSIVE`.
- Same control with `role="button"`, `aria-label="Send"`, and `aria-disabled="true"`: both checks pass. It remains visually disabled, with the same SVG and CSS.

This is **not a general claim that disabled buttons are filtered out**. Removing only the `disabled` CSS class does not make the original node appear in the observation. A native named `<button disabled>` is represented normally.

I understand that the original markup lacks accessible button semantics and that `alt` on an inline SVG is not a proper accessible name. The issue is the coverage gap for visible, non-semantic custom controls: the application can render the requested control while the model receives no representation of it.

Expected: either preserve a generic representation of such visible controls/graphics, or explicitly expose that the semantic observation cannot cover them so the judge can request visual evidence or report an observation limitation. The runner should not treat an omitted representation as evidence that the control is absent. I am not asking the model to infer a trustworthy button name from an arbitrary CSS class.

If this is an intentional limitation of semantic-only assertions, a documented diagnostic or fallback would help. The existing vision hint is useful; this report isolates the information lost before the model call.

