### Likely source of the omission

At commit `8d38206f460415b70706b45acb820bb0e24832ae`:

- [`isInteresting()` / `walk()`](https://github.com/tester-army/e2e/blob/8d38206f460415b70706b45acb820bb0e24832ae/packages/web/src/in-page/read-semantics.ts#L1189-L1261) retain nodes with semantic roles, accessible names, direct text, or test IDs, plus the empty painted-box special case. The unnamed wrapper has a child and no qualifying semantics.
- [`altOf()`](https://github.com/tester-army/e2e/blob/8d38206f460415b70706b45acb820bb0e24832ae/packages/web/src/in-page/read-semantics.ts#L393-L398) uses `alt` for `img` / `input[type=image]`, not inline SVG. An unnamed SVG is opaque, so its path data does not reach the model either.
- [`isDisabled()`](https://github.com/tester-army/e2e/blob/8d38206f460415b70706b45acb820bb0e24832ae/packages/web/src/in-page/read-semantics.ts#L1006-L1018) recognizes native/ARIA disabled state. An application CSS class alone is not that state.

The node omission is deterministic and can be inspected without a model via `e2e mcp` (`open_session`, then `observe` in the tool catalog). The exact LLM verdict is not the deterministic part of this report. Screenshots or proper application semantics are workarounds; replacing the agent assertion with a CSS assertion would avoid testing the Agent's judgment rather than address its missing evidence.

Adds `@e2e-dev/decision`, an executor that runs `agent.act` and `agent.assert` through an AI SDK decision model instead of an LLM. The decision model picks the next action from choices the executor builds out of the latest semantic tree; a small language model writes field text only when the choice is `type`. The runner still authorizes, budgets, and records every dispatch.

```ts
import { decisionExecutor } from '@e2e-dev/decision';
import { typeSafeAi } from '@ai-sdk/typesafe-ai';
import { openrouter } from '@openrouter/ai-sdk-provider';

export default {
  agents: {
    default: {
      executor: decisionExecutor({
        model: typeSafeAi.evaluationModel('jev-latest'),
        textModel: openrouter('inception/mercury-2.5'),
      }),
    },
  },
} satisfies E2EConfig;
```

Tests stay plain natural language, with no params:

```ts
await agent.act('add two todos, Buy milk and Walk the dog, then mark Buy milk as done');
```

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  O[observe tree] --> T[element table]
  T --> E["one experimental_evaluate call:<br/>operation + one target question per operation"]
  E -->|tap / check / select / submit / scroll / back| A[ctx.actions]
  E -->|type| X[text model writes the value] --> A
  E -->|done / failed| V[verdict on a fresh screen]
  A --> O
  V -->|confirmed| R[step verdict]
  V -->|rejected| O
```

- One `experimental_evaluate` call per action, with the target questions fanned out in the same request. An operation with one target dispatches without a target question. Every SDK call lives in `packages/decision/src/evaluate.ts`, so the `experimental_decide` rename in vercel/ai#21935 is a one-file change.
- Targets are element indexes from `packages/decision/src/elements.ts`: role rules per operation, viewport first, 255 choices per question, page text and the change fingerprint built from the tree without node ids. Model output never becomes a selector, URL, or key.
- `done` and `failed` go through one verdict question on a fresh screen that sees the step's action descriptions and no model reasoning. The second unconfirmed claim ends the step by the check: `fails` → `ACTION_FAILED`, anything else → `blocked`.
- Action errors go back to the model as history, matching the built-in loop; only `STEP_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED`, `STEP_TIMEOUT`, and `CANCELLED` propagate. Three actions with no page change block the step.
- `minProbability` and `minConfidence` are off by default; under a gate they cover operations, targets, secrets, completion checks, and assertions.
- Secrets stay declared handles: secret projections are stripped from params at any depth, password fields get only `typeSecret`, and a secret name appears only in the `secret` question's criteria.
- `cache: 'inherit'`: replays record typed values, and `ctx.replayedPrefix` seeds the history.
- `ai` is a required peer. No vendor transports, no vision, no `navigate`, no new error codes. Limits are in `docs/decision-models.mdx`.

## Benchmark

Testbed todo flow written in plain language (above), 5 `--no-cache` runs each on one M1 Mac. Both configurations finished every run.

| Executor | Models | Completed | Wall / run, mean (range) | Model calls / run | Tokens / run | Cost / run |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| built-in agent | `gpt-6-luna` | 5/5 | 17.3s (13.6–20.6) | 6 | 26.8k in (29–70% cached), 0.17k out | $0.0016 |
| `decisionExecutor` | Jev 1.13 decides, `gpt-6-luna` writes field text | 5/5 | 10.8s (9.5–12.6) | 9 (7 Jev + 2 text) | 17.2k Jev + 0.4k luna in | $0.0008 |

- The decision executor took the same path on all five runs: type "Buy milk", tap Add, type "Walk the dog", tap Add, check "Buy milk", `done`, confirmed by the verdict. The built-in agent also finished every run, with more latency spread.
- Where the 10.8s goes: 7 Jev calls at 0.42s each (about 3s), 2 text calls at 2.3s each (about 4.7s), the rest is the browser. A faster text model is the main lever.
- Costs: Jev from the `usage.cost` the gateway returned on each call ($0.042/M input, output unbilled), $0.0036 for all 35 calls. Luna at $0.10/M input, $0.01/M cached, $0.50/M output.
- Jev ran through Merge's System One gateway (`api-gateway.merge.dev/v1/decisions`, model `typesafe/jev-1.13`), not TypeSafe's API, since I have no TypeSafe credits. That gateway accepts structured `state` but only string instructions and criteria, so the bench adapter below stringifies those two fields.
- Luna is the text model because it was the only model reachable here that returned structured output; a smaller one such as Mercury should cut the text-call share.
- The configs are not committed (they need third-party keys).

