## Where it comes from

`@e2e-dev/web/dist/locators.js`, `glyphTolerantName`, turns a string role name into a RegExp with the
`u` flag (needed for `\p{Co}`). Playwright's `escapeRegexForSelector` does not escape quotes for a
`u` or `v` RegExp (`if (re.unicode || re.unicodeSets) return String(re);`, because `\'` is not a valid
escape in unicode mode). The apostrophe therefore reaches the attribute selector parser raw, opens a
quoted string, and the rest of the selector, including the `>> e2e-read=` part the engine appends, is
read inside it.

## Suggested fix

In `glyphTolerantName`, after escaping the regex metacharacters, write the three quote characters as
hex escapes, which are valid in unicode mode and keep the selector parseable:

```js
.map((word) => word
  .replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\/]/g, '\\$&')
  .replace(/'/g, '\\x27').replace(/"/g, '\\x22').replace(/`/g, '\\x60'))
```

The last probe above shows the effect. A caller-supplied `u`/`v` RegExp has the same problem; the same
rewrite could be applied to it before it is handed to `getByRole`, or the limitation documented.

## Workaround we use

We pass such names as a RegExp without the `u` flag, the apostrophe matched by `.`.

