### Problem

I use `agent.act` for the flow and `agent.assert` / `agent.waitFor` for the checks, and I want a run in which nothing changed to make no model calls. Today the `act` steps replay from the cache, but the judgments do not. `docs/cache.mdx` says: "`agent.assert`, `agent.waitFor`, and `agent.extract` always run live."

```ts
import { test } from 'e2e';

test('adds an item', async ({ app, agent }) => {
  await app.open('/cart');
  await agent.act('add one item to the cart');            // replays from the cache
  await agent.assert('the cart shows one item');          // asks the model on every run
  await agent.waitFor('the checkout button is enabled');  // asks the model whenever the screen changes
});
```

When every `act` replays, the judgments are the model calls this test still makes, and the only part of it that can differ between two identical runs.

I would like to ask whether "judgments always run live" is a deliberate guarantee, because I could not find where it is decided today:

- `packages/e2e/src/cache/identity.ts` says only `act` is cached because "judgments are made fresh per run by normative rule (spec 10)".
- Spec 10 was removed in #161, and `AGENTS.md` says "There is no separate spec. The code is the contract."
- In the deleted text (commit 02a382c), the reasons are that the assertions after a step own semantic drift, and that flushing the store can slow a run but never change a verdict.

Questions:

1. Is "a judgment always runs live" intended to stay? If so, I will not pursue this.
2. If an opt-in flag is acceptable, is `cache.assert` / `cache.waitFor` the right place?
3. Today `agent.assert` and `agent.waitFor` count as the verification that lets an `act` recording be written. Should a judgment that was itself replayed still count?

### Proposed API

```ts
// Test files do not change. The only change is three optional boolean keys on `CacheConfig`.
//
// e2e.config.ts
cache: {
  act: true,       // default true: today's behavior
  assert: false,   // default false: true lets a recorded `agent.assert` replace the model call
  waitFor: false,  // default false: true does the same for `agent.waitFor`
},

// Types (sketch), in packages/e2e/src/types.ts:
//
// export interface CacheConfig {
//   mode?: CacheMode;
//   store?: CacheStore;
//   dir?: string;
//   strict?: boolean;
//   act?: boolean;      // new, default true
//   assert?: boolean;   // new, default false
//   waitFor?: boolean;  // new, default false
// }
//
// What would be recorded is not the model's text. It is a locator expression plus an
// `expect(locator)` matcher, evaluated on the current screen.
//
// Replay answers only `holds`; anything else goes to the model. So the cache can never turn a
// pass into a failure, but it can, in the worst case, keep a pass that a fresh judgment would
// have failed. That is the cost, and the reason it is off by default.
//
// `extract` is not covered: its result is screen content, which docs/cache.mdx says a recording
// does not hold, and it flows into later test code. No `extract` key is offered.
//
// Compatibility (CONTRIBUTING, "Stability policy"): additive. With every new key at its default,
// config, behavior and the layout of .e2e/cache/ are unchanged. Judgment recordings would be
// written only when a flag is on, separate from the existing trace entries.
```

