Investigated and drafted with AI assistance. All runtime results came from deterministic local fixtures; no real model was called.

`device` is a closed method list. A host embedding e2e (T3 Code) or a test needing an agent-device command `device` doesn't wrap had no way to reach its own session: no device id, no session name, no client. The workaround was a second agent-device client guessing the session name `e2e-<target>-<n>`.

- `device.unstable_raw` returns the session name, the device's agent-device id and name, the engine's device selection, and an agent-device client bound to the session.
- Prefixed `unstable_`: no support promise, it may change or go away in any release.
- Device id and name come from the `boot` result in `init` (`packages/mobile/src/surface.ts`), so they are exact even when the pool let the daemon pick a booted device.
- `client` reads the worker's live client; after dispose it throws `INVALID_STATE`, like the rest of the surface.
- Raw calls are deliberately outside e2e: not steps (no report or trace entry), not cached or replayed, args not redacted, no settle or transition wait. JSDoc, the reference entry, and the guide say so.

```ts
test('reads its own session', async ({ device }) => {
  const { client, session, deviceId, selection } = device.unstable_raw;
  const own = (await client.sessions.list()).find((entry) => entry.name === session);
  await client.apps.list(selection);
});
```

```ts
/** The agent-device session behind `device`; see `Device.unstable_raw`. */
export interface DeviceSession {
  readonly platform: MobilePlatform;
  readonly session: string;
  readonly deviceId: string;
  readonly deviceName: string;
  readonly selection: DeviceSelection;
  readonly client: AgentDeviceClient;
}
```

