This differs from #1358, which concerns timestamp recognition in alerts. I am not proposing another clock-format pattern or a specific cache design.

The example does not edit a cache entry or use private framework helpers. AI assistance helped prepare this report. We ran the reproduction locally.

### What happened

A date written with an ordinal suffix (`October 26th`, `3rd October`) is not read as a date by `VOLATILE_TEXT` in `cache/anchors.ts`. The day pattern ends in `\b`, and there is no word boundary between the day digits and `th`, so the pattern does not match at all.

Two effects:

- An alert such as `Due October 26th` is not read as a placeholder. The next day's replay fails with `REPLAY_STALE (end-mismatch)` under `--strict-cache`, a hand-off otherwise, naming the recorded alert as missing and the new one as unrecorded.
- A non-alert node with such a date is not skipped as volatile, so it is recorded as an end anchor and goes stale the next day.

```text
/\b(?:jan|...|dec)[a-z]*\.?\s+\d{1,2}(?:,?\s+\d{4})?\b/i

"Due October 26"        month first: match    day first: no match
"Due October 26th"      month first: no match day first: no match
"Due October 3rd, 2026" month first: no match day first: no match
"Due 3rd October"       month first: no match day first: no match
"Due 3 October"         month first: no match day first: match
```

`October 26` is skipped as volatile today; only the suffixed spelling is missed. The fix would allow an optional `st|nd|rd|th` after the day in both date patterns. No new date shape is added.

