**Why it matters**

A command that brings up more than one service (a database plus a dev server, for example) can lose part of it mid-run, and the run still looks green. The config reference, under `command`, covers startup (`startupTimeout`, `APP_UNREACHABLE`) and shutdown (`shutdownTimeout`). It doesn't say what happens when the command exits in between.

Brings `browser.route` decisions in line with Playwright on three points.

- `route.fulfill({ body })` takes a `Buffer` or `Uint8Array` and sends the bytes unchanged (`packages/web/src/route-options.ts`, `requireBody`).
- `route.continue({ headers })` drops a header set to `undefined`, also on redirects. `fulfill` headers stay strings only.
- `route.fulfill` sets every cookie of a `\n`-joined `set-cookie`, the shape `route.fetch()` headers already report. Each line is checked on its own; `\r`, or a line break in any other header, is still refused as header injection.
- `WebResponse.body()` returns the bytes (`route.fetch()` and `waitForResponse`), so a fetched binary response can be fulfilled again. `text()` and `json()` decode from it.

```ts
await browser.route('**/report.pdf', async (route) => {
  const upstream = await route.fetch();
  await route.fulfill({
    headers: { ...upstream.headers, 'content-disposition': 'attachment' },
    body: await upstream.body(),
  });
});
await browser.route('**/api/**', (route) =>
  route.continue({ headers: { ...route.request.headers, authorization: undefined } }));
await browser.route('**/login', (route) =>
  route.fulfill({ headers: { 'set-cookie': 'a=b\nc=d' }, body: '' }));
```

