### Problem

On Windows, `sourceLocation` drops stack frames with native drive paths and `file:///D:/...` URLs. The shared parser requires a slash-prefixed location with no colon, so the drive letter prevents a match.

Failures then lose their project file/line and the list reporter's code frame. Recorded steps use the same parser.

### Reproduction

Use the public repository, https://github.com/e2e-inc/e2e, on Windows with Node 24. From its root, run:

```js
// Save as repro.mjs in the checkout, then run `node repro.mjs`.
import path from 'node:path';
import { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';
import { sourceLocation } from './packages/e2e/src/internal/source.ts';

const root = process.cwd();
const file = path.join(root, 'tests', 'checkout.e2e.ts');
for (const location of [file, pathToFileURL(file).href]) {
  console.log(sourceLocation(`Error: boom\n    at checkout (${location}:12:4)`, root));
}
```

Both calls print `undefined`. Expected for each:

```js
{ file: 'tests/checkout.e2e.ts', line: 12, column: 4 }
```

### Proposed fix

Recognize Windows drive paths and file URLs when extracting a stack location, preserving the existing project-root and `node_modules` exclusions.

`ObservationFeed.relocate()` resolves a stale node by always taking a fresh capture, even though the feed already keeps the last observations of the step:

```
packages/e2e/src/agent/observation-feed.ts:47:const MAX_RECENT_OBSERVATIONS = 8;
packages/e2e/src/agent/observation-feed.ts:265:  async relocate(stale: RedactedNode): ... {
  // ... descriptor match, then:
  const observation = await instrumentPhase(..., () => this.capture(false), ...);
```

The ring at `observation-feed.ts:86` ("The newest observations of the step, oldest first") already holds screens the stale node may still be on — if the node matched one or two observations ago, the fresh capture replays work the step already paid for: a full observe (engine snapshot + pixels budget) and a publish, on every relocation, inside the action's timeout.

Plan: a `perf(agent)` PR making `relocate()` search the recent ring for the descriptor first and only capture when no recent observation matches, keeping the current capture path as the fallback; behavior change kept to "same node, no extra screen" and covered by the existing `agent-input-stale` integration test plus a new unit case.

## What and why

A single serial group in a run tells little about how two of them behave together. The existing integration case (`runs serial groups as one unit on one worker`, `packages/e2e/tests/integration/parallel-workers.test.ts:140`) runs one `describe({ serial: true })` beside one parallel test, so nothing exercises two groups queueing against each other while a third unit competes for a worker. A scheduler regression in group dispatch would pass the suite that gates every pull request. The testbed has the same single-group shape, and on pull requests it runs only a model-picked subset, so the case belongs here in the integration suite.

- A new case runs two stateful serial groups, `alpha` and `beta`, in separate files so each is its own scheduling unit, plus a parallel neighbor, with two workers.
- Each group's second step checks a module counter that only survives when both steps ran in the same worker process in order, and every step writes its `process.pid` into a shared temp directory, so the test pins both properties directly: one worker per group, order preserved.
- The report assertion expects exactly two `serialGroups`, both `passed`, so a run that drops, merges, or reorders a group fails the case even when every step passed.

The test body mirrors the existing single-group case (same `workerFakeConfigSource(2)` setup, same module-state trick), so it reads as its sibling rather than a new idiom:

```ts
test.describe('alpha', { serial: true }, () => {
  let shared = 0;

  test('alpha first', async ({ app }) => {
    await app.open();
    shared += 1;
    writeFileSync(path.join(pidDir, 'alpha-first-' + process.pid), 'ok');
  });

  test('alpha second shares state', async ({ app }) => {
    if (shared !== 1) throw new Error('alpha module state was not preserved: ' + shared);
    await app.open();
    writeFileSync(path.join(pidDir, 'alpha-second-' + process.pid), 'ok');
  });
});
```

After the run the test groups the pid files per group and asserts each group's two steps recorded a single pid:

```ts
const pids = new Set(
  written.filter((entry) => entry.startsWith(`${group}-`)).map((entry) => entry.slice(entry.lastIndexOf('-') + 1)),
);
expect(pids.size, `${group} ran on ${pids.size} workers`).toBe(1);
```

