`--strict-cache` fails a step that was never recorded with `REPLAY_STALE` when its test id is longer than 300 characters and shares its first 299 characters with a test that has a recording of the same step.

## Reproduction

Built CLI from `main` at 2030b175, web engine, scripted model:

```ts
describe('<342-character describe title>', () => {
  test('first variant of the counter', async ({ app, agent, screen }) => {
    await app.open('/');
    await agent.act('increment the counter');
    await expect(screen.getByRole('status', { name: 'Counter' })).toHaveText('1');
  });
  test('second variant of the counter', async ({ app, agent, screen }) => {
    await app.open('/');
    await agent.act('increment the counter');
    await expect(screen.getByRole('status', { name: 'Counter' })).toHaveText('1');
  });
});
```

1. `e2e run --grep 'first variant'` in `read-write` mode records one entry, for the first test only.
2. `CI=1 e2e run --strict-cache` replays the first test and fails the second:

```
Tests  1 failed | 1 passed (2)
Cache  1 replayed · 1 missed
× |web| tests/long.e2e.ts:11 > checkout flow for a signed in customer ... > second variant of the counter
  REPLAY_STALE: the recording of this step no longer replays: the store holds it under another cache key (3ec5626f....json), since the runner, the engine, the app, or the agent's context changed after it was recorded, ...
```

The same project with the describe title `checkout flow` passes both tests: the second one runs live, as `docs/cache.mdx` promises for a step that was never recorded.

## Cause

An entry stores its step's provenance through `recordedProvenance` (`packages/e2e/src/cache/recorder.ts`), which cuts `testId`, `targetId`, and `agent` to 300 characters (`MAX_TRACE_DESCRIPTOR_CHARS`). The strict check for a recording under another key (`StoredRecordings.underAnotherKey` in `packages/e2e/src/cache/rekeyed.ts`) compares the step's cut provenance to each entry's. Two test ids that differ only after character 299 cut to the same string, so the second test's miss matches the first test's recording.

### Problem

A replayed `agent.act` step gives up on its recorded end state after about 14 s, however the project is configured. When the app answers slower than that on a replay, a correct recording fails with `REPLAY_STALE` under `--strict-cache`, and is handed to the agent otherwise. #1144 reported it:

> The replay then gives up with `end-mismatch`, even though the expected nodes show up shortly after.

#1144 was closed on 2026-11-13 after the request for a reproduction, and #1154, #1211 and #1319 are closed, so I found nothing open that tracks it. Here is the reproduction, on main f64e7dba: a checkout page whose confirmation shows `DELAY_MS` after the click, and a scripted executor in place of the model, so it needs no key. A recording made at 0.5 s stores an `endWaitMs` of about 10.7 s. The same entry replayed against a slower app:

| Run | App answers in | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--strict-cache` | 0.5 s | `Cache 1 replayed`, 1.3 s |
| `--strict-cache` | 8 s | `Cache 1 replayed`, 8.4 s |
| `--strict-cache` | 12 s | `Cache 1 replayed`, 14.4 s |
| `--strict-cache` | 15 s | `REPLAY_STALE` (end-mismatch) after 14.4 s |
| default | 15 s | `Cache 1 handed off`, the executor finishes the step, 15.8 s |

The stored wait is not what ends it. As the reviews on #1154 and #1211 found, `verifyEndState` first polls on the settle backoff (`RETRY_TIMEOUT_MS`, 15 s; its looks stop at about 14 s) and counts the recorded wait from the same start, so a stored wait under about 14 s adds nothing. Only a step that was already slow when recorded gets more.

The repro slows the app itself, not the machine. It shows the limit, not the load in #1144.

