Written with AI help (Claude Opus 5.5, Claude Fable 5.2). The runs above are real.

### Problem

I run one app build as several environments: signed in as an admin, signed in as a member, or in arm B of an experiment. Each environment is a target. When I run `e2e run --target ios-member`, I want two things:

1. Every device the run gives to `ios-member` is signed in as a member before its first test.
2. A test can tell which environment it runs in.

Neither works today. A target picks an engine and an app, and that's all:

- A test can't tell which target it runs on. The only fixture about it is `platform`, and two iOS targets both say `ios`.
- Nothing prepares a device before its first test. A `test.extend` fixture can get there, but only with workarounds (see Alternatives).
- Nothing runs when a target is done, so the temporary accounts are never deleted.

```ts
test('members can place an order', async ({ screen }) => {
  // Which target is this, and which account is this device signed in with?
  await expect(screen.getByText(`user #${userNo}`)).toBeVisible();
});
```

A/B arms, plan tiers, regions, and feature flags need the same thing: anything the app keeps per user or per install. #1855 left `globalSetup` and worker fixtures out until they have a design. This is a proposal for that part, scoped to targets.

### Proposed API

A target gets three new keys, and tests get `inject()`, as in Vitest:

- `provide`: values that describe the environment, such as `{ role: 'member' }`.
- `bootstrap`: puts each device into the environment before its first test.
- `teardown`: undoes it when the run ends.

```ts
interface Target {
  provide?: Readonly<Record<string, JsonValue>>;
  // Gets the fixtures a test gets, plus target { name, platform, slot }, runId, inject, provide(key, value), signal.
  bootstrap?(context: BootstrapContext): void | Promise<void>;
  // Gets target, runId, inject, signal. No device.
  teardown?(context: TeardownContext): void | Promise<void>;
}

// Widen ProvidedContext with declare module 'e2e'.
export function inject<K extends keyof ProvidedContext & string>(key: K): ProvidedContext[K];
```

Step 1 is `provide` and `inject()` alone. A test can already tell its target:

```ts
targets: [
  { name: 'ios-admin', engine: mobile({ platform: 'ios' }), app, provide: { role: 'admin' } },
  { name: 'ios-member', engine: mobile({ platform: 'ios' }), app, provide: { role: 'member' } },
],

test('members see the member home', async ({ screen }) => {
  test.skip(inject('role') !== 'member', 'member only');
  await expect(screen.getByText('Member home')).toBeVisible();
});
```

Step 2 adds `bootstrap` and `teardown`. Here each device gets its own temporary account and signs in through the app's UI:

```ts
const role = (name: 'admin' | 'member'): Target => ({
  name: `ios-${name}`,
  engine: mobile({ platform: 'ios' }),
  app,
  provide: { role: name },
  bootstrap: async ({ screen, target, runId, inject, provide, signal }) => {
    const account = inject('account') ?? (await createAccount(name, { key: `${runId}:${target.name}:${target.slot}`, signal }));
    provide('account', account); // the runner keeps it from here: the slot's teardown deletes it even if sign-in fails
    provide('userNo', account.userNo);
    // The same screen a test gets: on mobile() this drives the simulator through agent-device.
    await screen.getByTestId('email-input').fill(account.email);
    await screen.getByTestId('password-input').fill(account.password);
    await screen.getByTestId('sign-in-button').tap();
  },
  teardown: async ({ inject, signal }) => {
    await deleteAccount(inject('userNo'), { signal });
  },
});

export default { targets: [role('admin'), role('member')] } satisfies E2EConfig;
```

The test from the problem then reads `inject('userNo')`. An A/B target has the same shape, with one more call in bootstrap: `await assignExperiment(account, 'checkout', inject('variant'), { signal })`.

What the runner guarantees:

- Bootstrap runs once per worker slot (one device on a device engine). No test runs on that device until it passes.
- If bootstrap fails twice on a device, that target's tests are skipped. Other targets keep running.
- When a worker is replaced after a failed test or a crash, bootstrap runs again on the new one. `inject()` returns what the slot provided before, so bootstrap can reuse the account.
- Teardown runs in the runner at the end, once for every slot that provided anything, even if its worker crashed.
- A failed bootstrap leaves a trace page like a failed test does: its steps, the screen, and a screenshot. The run error links to it.

I have both steps working on a branch, with integration tests on the fake engine: https://github.com/gronxb/e2e/compare/main...rfc/target-bootstrap. The videos are two runs on the same two iOS simulators, one per role. In each, bootstrap creates an account on each simulator and signs in, the shared tests and that role's tests run (8 passed, 4 skipped for the other role or another demo scenario), and teardown deletes both accounts.

