### Upsides

- `--target` names an environment, and every device in it is ready before its first test, including one that replaces a crashed worker.
- One run covers every role or arm, with shared and role-only tests in one suite and one report.
- The app stays signed in from bootstrap into the tests, with no relaunch.
- `provide` and `inject` follow Vitest's names and shape.

### Downsides

- New public API: three target keys and an `inject` export. Workers get a new lifecycle stage.
- A passing bootstrap's steps aren't in the report. Only a failed one leaves a trace page.
- `inject()` doesn't work at a test file's top level, unlike Vitest. The runner collects each file once for all targets, before any device has bootstrapped.
- `ProvidedContext` is one type for all targets, so a key only some targets provide isn't typed per target.
- MCP `open_session` and `openHost` need the same wiring, or `inject()` throws there.

### Alternatives

- **A `test.extend` fixture, which is what I do today.** It works, but every part is a workaround. It finds its target by parsing the engine's unstable session name (`device.unstable_raw.session`). It keeps a target-to-role map in the test file. It doesn't know what state the device is in, so before every test it opens the app and walks the sign-in screens. With nothing to run at the end, the backend has to keep one account per target and slot forever. A failed sign-in fails every test instead of skipping the target.
- **A worker-scoped `auto` fixture**, as in Playwright's one-account-per-worker recipe. It runs inside the first test, so a failed sign-in fails tests one by one instead of skipping the target. Its teardown runs in the worker, so a crash leaves the account behind. Per-target values would need Playwright's per-project `use` options, which e2e doesn't have.
- **`test.setup`.** It saves sessions, but it isn't tied to a target or a device, and app memory state can't be saved as a session.
- **Launch arguments per target** (`app.launchArguments`). They pass a flag into the app, but can't create an account, assign an experiment arm, or clean up. Tests still can't read their role.
- **A callback in engine options**, such as `mobile({ bootstrap })`. Engine options cross the engine protocol as JSON, so a function can't reach a remote engine.
- **One `e2e run` per role, with an environment variable.** This loses the shared run, the single report, and the device pool.
- **A `targets` test option, as proposed in #1588.** It picks which tests run on a target, but doesn't pass values or prepare devices. It pairs with this: member-only tests could use `{ targets: ['ios-member'] }` instead of `test.skip(inject('role') !== 'member')`.

### Which surface

public types (SDK), config keys

### Compatibility

- [ ] This changes or removes something that exists today (see the stability policy in CONTRIBUTING.md).

`test.extend()` fixtures ran for every test registered through that `test`, named or not, so each setup needed its own exported `test`. `beforeAll` and `afterAll` saw `platform` only, and nothing could be set up once per worker: the mobile benchmark kept its one-install-per-device promise on `globalThis`. Closes ENG-1297.

## Breaking: fixtures are set up on demand

- A test sets up the fixtures its body, its `beforeEach` and `afterEach` hooks, and those fixtures name by destructuring their first parameter. A fixture nothing names no longer runs.
- Names are read from the function's source, as Playwright and Vitest do. `async (fixtures) => {}`, `async (_, use) => {}`, a rest property, and a bound function are a `COLLECTION_ERROR` with the file and line; a fixture that needs nothing writes `async ({}, use) => {}`.
- A fixture that exists for its side effect takes `auto: true`.
- A fixture can name the fixtures declared beside it in the same `extend()`, in any order; a cycle is a `COLLECTION_ERROR`.
- A name the target has no fixture for, and a suite hook or worker fixture that names a test fixture, fail at selection, before any engine starts. A test that does not run on a target is not checked there.

```ts
// before: two tests, one per setup
export const test = base.extend<{ project: string }>({ project: ... });
export const taskTest = test.extend<{ task: string }>({ task: ... });

// after: one test, task names project
export const test = base.extend<{ project: string; task: string }>({
  project: async ({ app, agent, screen }, use) => { ... },
  task: async ({ project, agent, screen }, use) => { ... },
});
```

## Worker fixtures, and `beforeAll` gets them

- `[fn, { scope: 'worker' }]`, typed with the second type argument of `extend`. Set up once per worker and target, the first time a test or suite hook names it, torn down when the worker ends, last set up first.
- `beforeAll` and `afterAll` receive worker fixtures: `platform`, the worker fixtures of their chain, and on a `'worker'` engine the engine's fixtures, `app`, and `screen`. `SuiteFixtures` and `SuiteHookFn` stay as deprecated aliases.
- Engines declare `isolation: 'worker'` (`defineEngine`, the protocol manifest, `engine-manifest-1` schema). `@e2e-dev/mobile` does: a device keeps its state between attempts.
- The mobile benchmark's `build` fixture is `{ scope: 'worker', auto: true }`; the `globalThis` map is gone.

```ts
export const test = base.extend<{}, { database: Database }>({
  database: [async ({}, use) => {
    const database = await startDatabase();
    await use(database);
    await database.stop();
  }, { scope: 'worker' }],
});

test.beforeAll(async ({ database, device }) => {
  await database.seed();
  await device.setPermission('camera', 'deny'); // a mobile target
});
```

## Extending a built-in fixture

- `extend({ app: async ({ app }, use) => { await app.open('/dashboard'); await use(app); } })` wraps a built-in, or an earlier fixture of the chain. Inside, the name is the fixture it extends; everyone else gets what it passes to `use`.
- It keeps the type, scope, and `auto` of what it extends; a `scope` or `auto` that differs is a `COLLECTION_ERROR` naming both. So a `device` override on mobile runs once per worker and reaches `beforeAll`.
- A name like a core fixture, an engine fixture, or an earlier `extend` was refused before.

## How it runs

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  C[collect: fixtureNames per body, hook, fixture] --> S[select: planAttempt / planSuiteHook per target]
  S --> W[worker: bindFixtures from the plan]
  W --> T{scope}
  T -- test --> A[set up in the attempt, torn down after afterEach]
  T -- worker --> P[WorkerFixturePool: one instance per definition, torn down in dispose]
  H[beforeAll / afterAll] --> E{engine isolation}
  E -- attempt --> PL[platform + worker fixtures]
  E -- worker --> Q{serial group or e2e mcp?}
  Q -- yes --> O[the open attempt]
  Q -- no --> N[short attempt of its own, results/suite-hooks]
  N -- cannot open / interrupted --> F[hooks still run; device, app, screen throw the reason]
```

- `packages/e2e/src/collect/fixture-names.ts` reads names; `fixture-plan.ts` resolves order, scope, auto, cycles, and whether code reaches the engine; `fixture-use.ts` runs the plan at selection for pairs that run.
- `packages/e2e/src/run/fixture-pool.ts` replaces `extended-fixtures.ts`. A worker fixture holds forwarders for `device`, `app`, and `screen`, so its steps land in whichever test or hook is running.
- `packages/e2e/src/run/suite-attempt.ts` opens the short attempt for suite hooks and worker teardowns on a `'worker'` engine. A failed open is one run error in phase `launch`.
- Core fixtures are one table, `CORE_FIXTURES` in `packages/core/src/run/fixtures.ts`, each with its scope; `app` and `screen` follow `engine.isolation`.

## Decisions left to the reviewer

- Peer floors: `@e2e-dev/mobile` keeps `e2e` and `@e2e-dev/core` at `>=0.23.2`, right only if this ships in 0.23.2. If 0.23.2 is cut first, raise both.
- Code a timed-out test leaves running keeps using a worker fixture's forwarders, which point at whatever runs next. Scoping them by async context breaks teardown code after `use()`, which resumes in the setup's context. Left as a follow-up.
- A worker fixture declared in a test file, not a module it imports, gets a new instance each time the file is imported again (retry, serial group, setup test); the old one stays set up until the worker ends. Documented; a collection warning is a possible follow-up.

